# Who must approve what.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FILE is separation of duties. Three DIFFERENT questions need three DIFFERENT
# approvers, and collapsing any two of them makes the control self-referential:
#
#   pipeline-maintainers   "What does the control do?"        -- owns the machinery
#   security-approvers     "May this finding pass?"           -- owns exceptions ONLY
#   deploy-approvers       "May this change reach prod?"      -- owns promotion
#
# WHY THEY CANNOT BE THE SAME TEAM
#
# An earlier version of this file gave security-approvers both `/dispositions/` AND
# `reusable-security.yml`. That is self-referential authority: if you can edit the gate, you never
# need a disposition -- you simply make it pass. The exception process becomes theatre, because the
# people governing it can redefine what "secure" means instead of requesting an exception to it.
#
# Likewise deploy approval must NOT implicitly grant a security exception. "This change may reach
# prod" and "this finding may pass" are different claims with different evidence.
#
# ROLES, NEVER PEOPLE. A pipeline routed to an individual becomes an undocumented API and needs
# reconstructive surgery when that person moves on. Populate the team; never hardcode a name. One
# person may occupy several roles in a small estate -- but that collapse must be DELIBERATE and
# visible here, not an accident of who happened to have permissions.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE ENFORCES NOTHING ON ITS OWN. CODEOWNERS *requests* a reviewer. Branch protection must
# REQUIRE review from code owners, or this is documentation pretending to be a control -- the same
# declared-versus-enforced gap this kit exists to eliminate. Verify it, do not assume it.

# ── The machinery: what the control DOES ────────────────────────────────────────
# Changing these changes what "secure" means for every consumer.
/.github/workflows/          @YOUR_ORG/pipeline-maintainers
/assurance-taxonomy.yaml     @YOUR_ORG/pipeline-maintainers
/scripts/                    @YOUR_ORG/pipeline-maintainers
/tests/                      @YOUR_ORG/pipeline-maintainers

# ── Policy: how hard it enforces ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Profiles decide adoption-vs-strict and which capabilities are required. That is a security
# decision about enforcement posture, so BOTH teams sign: maintainers for correctness, security for
# whether the posture is acceptable.
/profiles/                   @YOUR_ORG/pipeline-maintainers @YOUR_ORG/security-approvers

# ── Exceptions: may THIS finding pass ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Security approves exceptions and NOTHING ELSE. Deliberately no ownership of the gate itself.
/dispositions/                             @YOUR_ORG/security-approvers
/schemas/disposition.schema.json           @YOUR_ORG/security-approvers
/schemas/disposition-request.schema.json   @YOUR_ORG/security-approvers

# Requests are NOT owned by security -- anyone may open one. They are reviewed, not gate-kept at
# authorship. Gate-keeping the request instead of the approval just stops people asking, and a team
# that cannot ask for a review disables the workflow instead.

# ── Promotion: may this change reach an environment ─────────────────────────────
# Environment protection rules are configured in repository settings, not here. deploy-approvers is
# named as the required reviewer THERE. Listed in this comment so the third role is visible in the
# same place as the other two rather than living only in a settings page nobody reads.
#   @YOUR_ORG/deploy-approvers  -> required reviewers on the `prod` environment
